WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.037
Before we start. This is a learning demonstration, not investment advice. It values a real

00:00:06.037 --> 00:00:12.074
company, Asbury Automotive, for teaching only, from public filings, and nothing in it is a

00:00:12.074 --> 00:00:18.112
recommendation to buy or sell anything. The narration is my voice, synthesized with my consent.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:28.276
Last time we built the base case. Three filings, nineteen labelled assumptions, five years

00:00:28.276 --> 00:00:33.941
of statements that tie, and one answer: two hundred and ninety-two dollars a share

00:00:33.941 --> 00:00:39.607
for Asbury Automotive, the two ninety-one seventy-five we rounded, with eighty percent of that

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:45.273
value sitting after 2030. The shares traded near one hundred and eighty-three in mid-September 2026.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.590
Nobody on a desk stops there, and neither will you. A single number is

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.110
the start of the conversation, not the end. Today we take the base case

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.630
apart. First the discount rate, which we admitted was a round number, and which

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:04.151
we now measure. Then every operating assumption, one at a time and then together.

00:01:04.151 --> 00:01:08.671
Then the question that turns a model into an argument: what would have to be

00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:13.191
true for the market's price to be right? Then the pictures. And then the

00:01:13.191 --> 00:01:17.711
part nobody teaches: how you tell colleagues, how you answer them, and how you

00:01:17.711 --> 00:01:22.231
defend the work when someone senior pushes back. Learning demonstration, not investment advice. Same

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:26.751
rule as before: every number comes from a filing or from arithmetic you can see.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:35.983
Ten percent was a placeholder. The honest number starts with beta: how much this stock

00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:41.915
moves when the whole market moves. We take sixty-one month-end prices for Asbury and for the

00:01:41.915 --> 00:01:47.848
S and P 500, August 2021 to August 2026, turn them into sixty monthly returns, and

00:01:47.848 --> 00:01:53.781
ask one question: when the market moved one percent, how much did Asbury move, on average?

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.340
The answer is the slope of a line through sixty points, and it has

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.100
a formula you should know by heart: beta equals the covariance of the two

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.860
return series divided by the variance of the market's returns. Here it is zero

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.620
point seven one. Read the rest of the grid, because the rest is where

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.380
the judgment lives. Asbury's monthly volatility is ten point three percent against the market's

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:23.141
four point six; the stock moves more than twice as much as the market.

00:02:23.141 --> 00:02:27.901
But the correlation is only zero point three two, so most of that movement

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:32.661
is Asbury's own, not the market's. That is why the beta comes out below

00:02:32.661 --> 00:02:37.421
one despite the volatility. The next line: the standard error, the statistical uncertainty of

00:02:37.421 --> 00:02:42.181
that slope, is zero point two eight. The usual convention is two standard errors

00:02:42.181 --> 00:02:46.941
either side, which puts the true beta anywhere from about zero point two to

00:02:46.941 --> 00:02:51.701
one point three. And R-squared is zero point one zero: the market explains ten

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:56.461
percent of Asbury's monthly moves. Ninety percent is company and industry. The last line

00:02:56.461 --> 00:03:01.221
is the industry's fix for exactly this problem. The Blume adjustment shrinks a measured

00:03:01.221 --> 00:03:05.981
beta toward one, two-thirds of the estimate plus one-third of one, which gives zero

00:03:05.981 --> 00:03:10.741
point eight one. Most data vendors quote you the adjusted number without saying so.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.418
Here is the picture. Every dot is one month. The red line

00:03:15.418 --> 00:03:19.295
is the beta. Look at how far the dots sit from the

00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:23.173
line. That scatter is the standard error, and you should carry it

00:03:23.173 --> 00:03:27.051
in your head every time someone quotes a beta to two decimal places.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:32.933
From beta to a discount rate is one line of arithmetic. The cost of

00:03:32.933 --> 00:03:38.015
equity is the risk-free rate plus beta times the equity risk premium: what investors

00:03:38.015 --> 00:03:43.098
demand over a government bond for holding an average stock. Ten-year Treasury, five percent.

00:03:43.098 --> 00:03:48.180
Equity risk premium, four point four two, the implied premium published in July. Observed

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.263
beta, five plus zero point seven one times four point four two: eight point one

00:03:53.263 --> 00:03:58.346
five percent. Blume-adjusted, eight point five seven. A beta of one, the market itself,

00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:03.428
nine point four two. And the bottom line, highlighted: the ten percent we used

00:04:03.428 --> 00:04:08.511
in Video 1 quietly assumed a beta of one point one three, higher than

00:04:08.511 --> 00:04:13.593
every measurement on this screen. We were conservative. Now you can see by how much.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:19.586
One regression on one stock is noisy. The desk fix is to borrow

00:04:19.586 --> 00:04:24.783
information from the peers. Four public dealer groups: AutoNation, Lithia, Group 1, Penske. Their

00:04:24.783 --> 00:04:29.979
measured betas run from zero point seven one to one point two two. But

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:35.176
each carries its own debt load, and leverage makes equity riskier, so before

00:04:35.176 --> 00:04:40.372
you average them you strip the leverage out. Unlevered beta is levered beta divided

00:04:40.372 --> 00:04:45.568
by one plus one minus the tax rate times debt over equity. Average the

00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:50.765
four unlevered betas: zero point five eight. That is the risk of running car

00:04:50.765 --> 00:04:55.961
dealerships, before anyone borrows. Then put Asbury's own leverage back: debt over equity

00:04:55.961 --> 00:05:01.157
of one point zero three, and the relevered beta is one point zero three.

00:05:01.157 --> 00:05:06.354
Cost of equity, nine point five four percent. Four noisy estimates averaged beat one

00:05:06.354 --> 00:05:11.550
noisy estimate; that is the whole point. Two rules on the way through, because

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.747
people get both wrong. First: the discount rate always wants the levered beta,

00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:21.943
at the leverage you are forecasting. A regression beta is already levered. Unlevering is

00:05:21.943 --> 00:05:27.139
only the bridge that lets you move a beta between companies. Second, and specific

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:32.336
to this industry: floor plan, the inventory loans from Video 1. We excluded it

00:05:32.336 --> 00:05:37.532
from debt for every one of these companies, because our model treats it

00:05:37.532 --> 00:05:42.728
as an operating liability everywhere else. Include it when you unlever and exclude it

00:05:42.728 --> 00:05:47.925
when you relever and you have manufactured a lower beta and a higher value

00:05:47.925 --> 00:05:53.121
out of an inconsistency. Same treatment on both legs, or the discount rate lies.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.135
Now the payoff. The five years of statements do not change. Only the rate

00:05:59.135 --> 00:06:04.350
does. At the observed beta, eight point one five percent, the value is three hundred

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:09.566
and ninety-nine dollars, a hundred and seven more than the base. Blume-adjusted, three sixty-nine. Nine

00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:14.781
percent, three forty-two. Beta of one, three nineteen. The bottom-up peer beta, three thirteen.

00:06:14.781 --> 00:06:19.996
Our ten percent, two ninety-two. Eleven percent, two fifty-four. Read the after-2030 column too: at

00:06:19.996 --> 00:06:25.211
the lower rates, eighty-four percent of the value is terminal. The cheaper the money, the

00:06:25.211 --> 00:06:30.426
more of the answer sits in the far future. Notice that every one of

00:06:30.426 --> 00:06:35.642
those rates is defensible, and the spread between them is a hundred and forty-five dollars

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:40.857
a share. That is larger than any operating disagreement you will have about this company.

00:06:40.857 --> 00:06:46.072
When someone asks you why the number moved, the first place to look is here.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:51.960
The rate has a partner: terminal growth. Nine rows, three rates by three growth

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.050
rates. Down a column, half a point of terminal growth is worth about sixteen dollars

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:02.139
at our ten percent, more at lower rates. Across a row, one point of discount

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:07.229
rate is worth thirty-four to fifty-seven dollars, depending on where you stand. Both are judgments.

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:12.319
Neither is in a filing. You show them as a grid, never as a point.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.501
One more screen on the terminal value, because eighty percent of the answer

00:07:17.501 --> 00:07:21.882
lives there and every line of it is a choice. Our base holds debt

00:07:21.882 --> 00:07:26.262
flat after 2030: the company refinances what matures, pays interest, and never repays

00:07:26.262 --> 00:07:30.643
principal. That is what going concerns do. It is also the conservative reading, because

00:07:30.643 --> 00:07:35.024
a company growing two and a half percent a year with a constant

00:07:35.024 --> 00:07:39.405
debt ratio would borrow a little more each year, and that borrowing is cash

00:07:39.405 --> 00:07:43.786
the owners do not have to put in. Borrow a third of each

00:07:43.786 --> 00:07:48.167
year's reinvestment, which is the Damodaran form: three sixteen. Let debt grow in step

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:52.547
with the firm: three twenty-four. Both defensible. We stay flat, and we say so.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:58.604
The same screen shows the other direction, which for a car dealer is the

00:07:58.604 --> 00:08:03.858
one the market is thinking about. If the industry stops growing, terminal growth zero:

00:08:03.858 --> 00:08:09.112
two twenty-nine. If it shrinks one percent a year forever: two twelve. Hold that

00:08:09.112 --> 00:08:14.366
thought for the price-implied table in a moment. And one accounting convention, flagged by

00:08:14.366 --> 00:08:19.620
our reviewers: the model saves thirty-one million of tax a year on the impairment charge,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.873
a book write-down. In practice that is usually a deferred-tax item with no cash

00:08:24.873 --> 00:08:30.127
saving. Take it away and the value is two seventy-one. We keep the settled

00:08:30.127 --> 00:08:35.381
convention, and we put the twenty-one dollars on the screen so nobody has to

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:40.635
take our word for it. A note on that reinvestment line, because it is

00:08:40.635 --> 00:08:45.889
the honest caveat about our own growth number. In 2030 the company reinvests one fifty-five

00:08:45.889 --> 00:08:51.143
out of five twenty-seven of net income, about twenty-nine percent, and we grow the

00:08:51.143 --> 00:08:56.397
cash flow at two and a half percent forever. That implies an eight and

00:08:56.397 --> 00:09:01.651
a half percent return on what it retains, below the ten percent cost of

00:09:01.651 --> 00:09:06.905
equity. In other words our terminal growth destroys a little value at the margin.

00:09:06.905 --> 00:09:12.159
That is conservative, and it is the kind of thing you disclose rather than hide.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:20.166
Now the business itself. Nine assumptions, each moved by a plausible amount

00:09:20.166 --> 00:09:24.872
in the bad direction and the good direction, everything else held at

00:09:24.872 --> 00:09:29.578
base, and the model rerun each time. Sorted by how much they matter.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:36.074
Gross margin, one point either way: two fifty-six to three twenty-seven, a seventy-one dollar

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:41.767
swing. One point of gross margin on roughly eighteen billion of revenue is roughly

00:09:41.767 --> 00:09:47.461
a hundred and eighty million of gross profit; it is worth as much as

00:09:47.461 --> 00:09:53.154
a full point of discount rate. Capital spending, fifty million a year either way:

00:09:53.154 --> 00:09:58.848
sixty-seven dollars, because every dollar not spent is a dollar to the owners, every year,

00:09:58.848 --> 00:10:04.541
and then carried into the terminal value. Organic growth, one point: forty-four dollars. The

00:10:04.541 --> 00:10:10.235
cost ratio, SG and A over gross profit, one point along the whole path:

00:10:10.235 --> 00:10:15.928
thirty-four. The term-debt rate: thirty-one. The cost ratio after 2030 only: twenty-five. Floor-plan rate:

00:10:15.928 --> 00:10:21.622
twenty-two. Impairment, forty million either way: fourteen, and notice it runs backwards, because a

00:10:21.622 --> 00:10:27.315
bigger non-cash charge lowers taxes and raises cash flow. Inventory days, five either way: ten.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:32.938
The same table as a picture, which is how you will show it in

00:10:32.938 --> 00:10:37.756
a meeting. Red is the bad direction, green the good, the line is the

00:10:37.756 --> 00:10:42.574
base. The eye goes to the top two bars, and it should: margin and

00:10:42.574 --> 00:10:47.393
capital spending are where the operating argument lives. Everything below the fourth bar is detail.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:53.311
Growth deserves its own screen, because one point eight percent was the judgment call

00:10:53.311 --> 00:10:58.432
in Video 1 and you should see what the alternatives are worth. No organic

00:10:58.432 --> 00:11:03.553
growth at all, revenue flat at eighteen billion for five years: two fifty-three, thirty-eight

00:11:03.553 --> 00:11:08.674
dollars below base. The same-store rate the stores actually posted in 2025, one point

00:11:08.674 --> 00:11:13.795
one five percent: two seventy-eight, fourteen below. Our one point eight: two ninety-two. Three

00:11:13.795 --> 00:11:18.916
percent, roughly nominal growth in the economy: three nineteen, twenty-seven above. And the reported

00:11:18.916 --> 00:11:24.037
four point seven, the growth that was bought: three fifty-nine, sixty-seven above base, for

00:11:24.037 --> 00:11:29.158
revenue the company does not own yet. Each point of growth is worth about

00:11:29.158 --> 00:11:34.279
twenty-two dollars here, less than a point of margin, because floor plan funds most

00:11:34.279 --> 00:11:39.401
of the working capital that growth needs. So the growth judgment matters, but it

00:11:39.401 --> 00:11:44.522
is not where the valuation lives; it lives in margin, capital spending, and the rate.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:49.667
The two operating judgments together. Margin down a point and the cost ratio

00:11:49.667 --> 00:11:54.014
up a point: two hundred and forty. Both the other way: three forty-five.

00:11:54.014 --> 00:11:58.360
Over a hundred dollars from two one-point moves. This grid is what you

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:02.707
hand to the colleague who says "I think margins are going lower and

00:12:02.707 --> 00:12:07.054
the integration is going worse"; you point to a cell instead of arguing.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:13.028
Single shocks are how you understand the model. Named cases are how you talk

00:12:13.028 --> 00:12:18.206
about the company. The bear case: the cost ratio never recovers, stuck at sixty-eight percent

00:12:18.206 --> 00:12:23.384
of gross profit. Two thirty-six. The bull case: the savings from combining the groups

00:12:23.384 --> 00:12:28.562
arrive fast, sixty-three percent. Three twenty-one. And a recession story: margin down a point, organic

00:12:28.562 --> 00:12:33.740
growth down a point, costs up a point, floor-plan rate up a point, all

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:38.918
at once. Two hundred and ten. Read the two right-hand columns as well. Every case

00:12:38.918 --> 00:12:44.096
is a full rerun and every balance sheet balances. And the recession story is

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:49.274
the first case in which cash hits the twenty-five million floor and the revolver is

00:12:49.274 --> 00:12:54.452
drawn, twelve million at the peak. That is the safety net from Video 1

00:12:54.452 --> 00:12:59.630
doing its job, and it is the kind of detail a stress case is for.

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:04.805
Now read the last line, because it is the mistake I see most

00:13:04.805 --> 00:13:09.179
often. If you had added the four single shocks from the tornado, you would

00:13:09.179 --> 00:13:13.554
have written down two oh seven. The model says two ten. Shocks interact; lower

00:13:13.554 --> 00:13:17.929
margin means lower inventory means lower floor plan means lower interest, and so on

00:13:17.929 --> 00:13:22.303
through every link we built in Video 1. Never add sensitivities. Rerun the model.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:27.297
Finally, the discipline that separates an analyst from a cheerleader: write down, in

00:13:27.297 --> 00:13:31.494
advance, what would make you withdraw the case. Three triggers, each tied to

00:13:31.494 --> 00:13:35.691
a number the next quarterly report will either confirm or deny. Gross margin

00:13:35.691 --> 00:13:39.888
at or below sixteen point zero five: value falls to two fifty-six. The cost

00:13:39.888 --> 00:13:44.085
ratio a point higher than our path, ending at sixty-five and a half:

00:13:44.085 --> 00:13:48.282
two seventy-five. Capital spending at or above three hundred: two fifty-eight. If the

00:13:48.282 --> 00:13:52.479
next two quarters show any of these, the base case is not patched

00:13:52.479 --> 00:13:56.676
one number at a time. It is withdrawn and rebuilt as a combined case.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:04.384
Here is the question that turns a model into an argument. The market

00:14:04.384 --> 00:14:08.788
says one eighty-three. We say two ninety-two. Instead of asking who is right,

00:14:08.788 --> 00:14:13.192
ask: what would have to be true for one eighty-three to be exactly

00:14:13.192 --> 00:14:17.596
right? Take one assumption at a time, hold the other eighteen at base,

00:14:17.596 --> 00:14:22.000
and search for the value that makes the model equal the price. The

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.404
search is a bisection: halve the interval until the model lands on one eighty-three.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.115
Cost of equity: thirteen point eight percent. Run that back through the formula and

00:14:31.115 --> 00:14:35.030
it is a beta of about two, when nothing we measured is above one

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:38.946
point three. Terminal growth: minus three point three percent, a business shrinking forever. Gross margin:

00:14:38.946 --> 00:14:42.861
fourteen point one percent every year, three points below the lowest year in the

00:14:42.861 --> 00:14:46.776
history. The cost ratio: six point two points higher than our path, every year, worse

00:14:46.776 --> 00:14:50.691
than any year the company has posted. Capital spending: four hundred and seven million

00:14:50.691 --> 00:14:54.607
a year, against guidance of two-fifty. Read this the right way. None of these is

00:14:54.607 --> 00:14:58.522
a forecast, and the truth is probably several of them partly at once. What

00:14:58.522 --> 00:15:02.437
the table tells you is the size of the disagreement in operating language. The market

00:15:02.437 --> 00:15:06.352
is not pricing a slightly worse Asbury; it is pricing a dealer that either

00:15:06.352 --> 00:15:10.268
never recovers its cost ratio or has to spend far more than it says to

00:15:10.268 --> 00:15:14.183
stand still. That is a claim you can test with two quarters of filings.

00:15:14.183 --> 00:15:18.098
Which is exactly the posture this course takes: wait for proof of the operating recovery.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:26.157
Three pictures carry the whole story, and you have already seen one of

00:15:26.157 --> 00:15:30.914
them, the tornado. The second is the simplest picture in finance and the

00:15:30.914 --> 00:15:35.670
one most people forget to draw: one axis. Price, one eighty-three. Bear, two

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:40.427
thirty-six. Base, two ninety-two. Bull, three twenty-one. And out on the right, three

00:15:40.427 --> 00:15:45.184
ninety-nine, what the observed beta alone would justify. Now anyone in the room

00:15:45.184 --> 00:15:49.941
can see in one second that the price sits below the bear case,

00:15:49.941 --> 00:15:54.697
and that the discount rate moves the answer further than the operating cases do.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:59.922
The third is the rate-and-growth grid as a heat map, wider than before: eight

00:15:59.922 --> 00:16:04.344
rates from eight point one five to twelve, five growth rates. Every cell is

00:16:04.344 --> 00:16:08.766
the same five years of statements. The base case is the bold cell. The

00:16:08.766 --> 00:16:13.188
darker the blue, the higher the value; the pattern runs diagonally, which tells you

00:16:13.188 --> 00:16:17.611
the two inputs trade off against each other. This is the picture for the

00:16:17.611 --> 00:16:22.033
colleague who says your terminal value is a guess. You agree, and you show

00:16:22.033 --> 00:16:26.455
them the whole surface instead of a point. Two rules for every picture. One

00:16:26.455 --> 00:16:30.877
number per picture, said in the title. And never a picture the model did

00:16:30.877 --> 00:16:35.299
not produce; every chart here is drawn from the same function that printed the grids.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:43.239
Now the part of the job that happens away from the model. Somebody senior

00:16:43.239 --> 00:16:47.878
is going to ask what you think about Asbury, and they will ask in a

00:16:47.878 --> 00:16:52.516
hallway, not a meeting. You need three versions of the answer, and you prepare

00:16:52.516 --> 00:16:57.155
all three before anyone asks. The two-line message. Base case two ninety-two on free cash

00:16:57.155 --> 00:17:01.794
flow to equity at ten and two and a half; shares near one eighty-three.

00:17:01.794 --> 00:17:06.433
The gap is the cost recovery and the discount rate; a point of margin or

00:17:06.433 --> 00:17:11.072
capex at three hundred takes thirty-three to thirty-six dollars off. Posture: wait for proof

00:17:11.072 --> 00:17:15.711
of the operating recovery in the next two quarters. That is it. Value, price, what

00:17:15.711 --> 00:17:20.349
drives the gap, what breaks it, where you stand. Anyone who wants more will ask.

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:26.096
The one page you attach: the assumption grids with their labels, the five-year statements

00:17:26.096 --> 00:17:31.042
with the check block under them, the rate table, the tornado, the what-would-have-to-be-true table,

00:17:31.042 --> 00:17:35.988
the three triggers, and the disclaimer. Nothing you have not already seen today. If

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:40.933
a colleague can reproduce your number from one page, you have done the work properly.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:46.397
And the fifteen-minute walk-through, in this order: posture first, then the answer and the price,

00:17:46.397 --> 00:17:51.064
then the three judgments that carry it, then what breaks it, then what you are watching

00:17:51.064 --> 00:17:55.732
for, then questions. Posture first, always. If people know where you stand before you show

00:17:55.732 --> 00:18:00.399
a number, they listen to the number. If they have to guess, they argue with it.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:08.589
You will get these eight questions. Have the number ready for each. Why ten

00:18:08.589 --> 00:18:13.478
percent? A starting convention; the measured beta is zero point seven one, the peer

00:18:13.478 --> 00:18:18.367
beta one point zero three; that range is worth three thirteen to three ninety-nine;

00:18:18.367 --> 00:18:23.255
ten percent is the conservative end. Eighty percent after 2030, isn't that a guess?

00:18:23.255 --> 00:18:28.144
It is true of every discounted cash flow of a company that keeps operating; that

00:18:28.144 --> 00:18:33.033
is why you showed the grid and tested the operating shocks separately. Reported growth

00:18:33.033 --> 00:18:37.922
is four point seven, why model one point eight? Because four point seven was

00:18:37.922 --> 00:18:42.811
bought; stores owned both years grew one point two; acquisitions get priced separately. Why

00:18:42.811 --> 00:18:47.700
is two billion of floor plan not debt? Because it is inventory funding from manufacturers'

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:52.589
finance arms and banks that moves one-for-one with inventory; you treat it as operating

00:18:52.589 --> 00:18:57.477
everywhere, and you showed what happens if you remove the line: cash at minus

00:18:57.477 --> 00:19:02.366
one point one billion. What is the market saying? Hold everything else and one

00:19:02.366 --> 00:19:07.255
eighty-three implies a cost of equity near fourteen, or a margin near fourteen, or

00:19:07.255 --> 00:19:12.144
capex over four hundred, or shrinking forever. None is your base; each is a hypothesis

00:19:12.144 --> 00:19:17.033
to test. What would make you drop the case? The three triggers, with their

00:19:17.033 --> 00:19:21.922
values. Why cash flow to equity, not to the whole firm? Because a dealer's

00:19:21.922 --> 00:19:26.811
financing is inseparable from its operations; valuing the equity directly with a levered beta

00:19:26.811 --> 00:19:31.699
avoids valuing the whole firm and subtracting debt, which would force you to decide whether

00:19:31.699 --> 00:19:36.588
floor plan is debt. Why not just use the data vendor's numbers? Because its

00:19:36.588 --> 00:19:41.477
capital-spending field said nineteen million for 2025 and the filing says two hundred and

00:19:41.477 --> 00:19:46.366
five. Every load-bearing number gets checked. Notice that every answer is a number plus

00:19:46.366 --> 00:19:51.255
the reason for it. That is what professional sounds like. Not confidence. Numbers with reasons.

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:59.199
If you are asked to present, six slides, in this order. Posture: wait

00:19:59.199 --> 00:20:03.847
for proof, not a recommendation. The answer and the price: two ninety-two base, two

00:20:03.847 --> 00:20:08.496
thirty-six to three twenty-one bear to bull, shares near one eighty-three. The three judgments

00:20:08.496 --> 00:20:13.145
that carry the valuation, in operating language, each with its evidence: organic growth of

00:20:13.145 --> 00:20:17.793
one point eight, the cost ratio recovering from sixty-six and a half to sixty-four

00:20:17.793 --> 00:20:22.442
and a half, and a ten percent cost of equity against a measured range

00:20:22.442 --> 00:20:27.090
of eight to nine and a half. What moves it: the tornado and the

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:31.739
rate-growth grid. What the price is paying for: the implied table. And what you

00:20:31.739 --> 00:20:36.388
are watching: the three triggers and the next two quarterly reports. Six slides, fifteen

00:20:36.388 --> 00:20:41.036
minutes, and every number on them is on a screen you have seen today.

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:46.626
You will be challenged twice, every time, and you should welcome both. "Your

00:20:46.626 --> 00:20:51.412
terminal value is the whole answer." Yes. Eighty percent. Here is the grid. And

00:20:51.412 --> 00:20:56.199
here is the operating case that has to hold for that terminal value

00:20:56.199 --> 00:21:00.985
to be earned: seventeen percent gross margin, a sixty-four and a half percent cost

00:21:00.985 --> 00:21:05.771
ratio, two hundred and fifty million of capital spending, nine hundred and seventy-one million

00:21:05.771 --> 00:21:10.557
of operating income in 2030. If those are reasonable for a company that,

00:21:10.557 --> 00:21:15.343
as you saw in Video 1, earned eight sixty-one in 2025, so is the

00:21:15.343 --> 00:21:20.130
terminal value. If they are not, say which one, and we will rerun it

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:24.916
together. "The market disagrees with you by a hundred and eight dollars." The

00:21:24.916 --> 00:21:29.702
market is a price, not an argument. Here are the single-assumption explanations of that

00:21:29.702 --> 00:21:34.488
price. Here is the one we think is wrong, the cost recovery, and

00:21:34.488 --> 00:21:39.275
here is the evidence that would change our mind: two quarters of the cost

00:21:39.275 --> 00:21:44.061
ratio. That is the whole method. Build it from the filings, label every judgment,

00:21:44.061 --> 00:21:48.847
break it on purpose, measure the rate, shock everything, ask what the price

00:21:48.847 --> 00:21:53.633
is paying for, draw three pictures, and stand behind a posture with numbers and

00:21:53.633 --> 00:21:58.419
reasons. Learning demonstration, not investment advice. Now go do it for your own company.
